 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Twenty non-inverting buffers in two banks of ten. Each bank has two active-low enables, OE1 and OE2: the bank's Y outputs follow their A inputs only while both enables are low, and go to high impedance when either is high."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply range",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The part works from 1.2 V to 3.6 V VCC; its design target is 1.65 V to 3.6 V. It is rated from –40°C to 85°C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Dynamic output control (DOC)",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "During an edge the output starts at low impedance to drive the load hard, then its impedance rises to cut noise, without costing speed. At the start of a transition the drive matches a high-drive standard output, equivalent to IOH and IOL of ±24 mA (stated both at 2.5 V and at 3.3 V VCC). The VOL–IOL and VOH–IOH curves show the output impedance and drive. TI application reports SCEA006 and SCEA009 cover the AVC family and DOC circuitry."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up and power-down",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A pull-up resistor from OE to VCC keeps the outputs at high impedance while VCC ramps up or down. The current the OE driver can sink sets the resistor's minimum value."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Partial power-down",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Ioff circuitry turns the outputs off when VCC is removed, so current cannot flow back through the part; power-off leakage is specified at ±10 µA with 3.6 V on an input or output."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Mixed voltages",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Inputs and outputs tolerate voltages above VCC, up to 3.6 V on an input or a disabled output, allowing mixed-voltage systems."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Speed and robustness",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Maximum propagation delay is under 2 ns at 2.5 V and 3.3 V VCC. Latch-up immunity exceeds 100 mA per JESD 78, Class II. Built on the EPIC submicron CMOS process; a Widebus family member."
  }
 ],
